From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] crypt32: countersignatures and CryptEnumOIDFunction

- CryptMsgCountersignEncoded makes the signer info of a countersigner over
  another signer's signature value: a detached signed message of those bytes,
  of which the signer is the result.
- CryptMsgCountersign does that for a signer of a decoded signed message and
  adds the result to the signer's unauthenticated attributes as a counterSign
  attribute (CMSG_CTRL_ADD_SIGNER_UNAUTH_ATTR, now understood by decoded
  signed messages: the attribute shows in CMSG_SIGNER_UNAUTH_ATTR_PARAM and
  CMSG_ENCODED_SIGNER; the message's own encoding is unchanged).
- CryptMsgVerifyCountersignatureEncoded(Ex), which only returned FALSE, check
  a countersignature against a public key, a certificate or a chain: the
  digest attribute when there are authenticated attributes (CRYPT_E_HASH_VALUE
  when it does not match), then the signature over the attributes or the
  signature value. CMSG_VERIFY_SIGNER_NULL checks the digest only.
- CryptEnumOIDFunction goes through the registered OID functions (the
  registry's OID tree) for an encoding type, function name and OID, each or
  any, handing the callback each entry's values; numeric OIDs ("#n") come as
  numbers; a callback that returns FALSE stops it.
